---
/**
 * The invitation, repeated after a section that made a case
 * (kolonie-website#87).
 *
 * The landing page is about 11,000 pixels tall and its calls to action were all
 * at the top. *"A reader convinced by an argument two-thirds of the way down has
 * to scroll back to act on it."* `#87` calls repeating the invitation the
 * cheapest conversion change available, and the reason is that a reader acts at
 * the moment of being convinced rather than several screens later.
 *
 * ## What it is, and what it deliberately is not
 *
 * `#87`: *"After every section that makes a case, one line and one button.
 * **Not a banner, not a repeated hero** — a single sentence that names what the
 * reader has just been persuaded of, and the action."*
 *
 * So this component is a sentence and a button, and there is nothing else in it.
 * It has no heading, no border and no background: eight of these down one page
 * is a rhythm if each is a line, and a page of boxes if each is a card.
 *
 * **The sentence is the caller's and it is not optional.** Naming what *this*
 * reader has just been persuaded of is the whole of the device — the same
 * sentence eight times is a repeated hero, which is the thing `#87` refused by
 * name.
 *
 * ## Where the button goes
 *
 * `#send-your-agent`, the closing block — which carries both ways to act, the
 * prompt and the install panel, side by side. Pointing back at the hero's panel
 * would send a convinced reader to the top of an 11,000-pixel page, which is the
 * complaint this issue is named after, in the other direction.
 *
 * ## The label
 *
 * `Send your agent` describes what a **new** visitor is about to do, which is
 * `#87`'s requirement and the reason `Sign in` stopped being the primary action
 * on this site. It is also the label the hero already uses, so the page has one
 * primary action rather than a vocabulary of them.
 *
 * The quiet *Already have an account?* line belongs beneath the **primary**
 * action and is in the hero, once. Repeating it under all eight would be the
 * banner this is not.
 */

import Button from './Button.astro'

interface Props {
  /**
   * Where the button goes. Defaults to the closing block, and a caller should
   * have a reason to change it.
   */
  href?: string
  /** The label. Defaults to the site's one primary action. */
  label?: string
}

const { href = '#send-your-agent', label = 'Send your agent' } = Astro.props
---

<p class="cta">
  <span class="cta__line"><slot /></span>
  <Button href={href} variant="primary" class="cta__act">{label}</Button>
</p>

<style>
  /* Tokens only — no colour value in this file, and `theme.test.ts` fails on
     one. */

  /* A row where there is width and a stack where there is not, without a
     breakpoint: the sentence takes what it needs and the button drops beneath
     it when what is left cannot hold it. The same device the card grids use. */
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--k-space-3) var(--k-space-4);
    margin: var(--k-flow) 0 0;
  }

  /* Deliberately not `--k-measure`. This is one line naming one thing; a
     sentence here that needed a reading measure would be a paragraph, which is
     what `#87` means by *not a banner*. */
  .cta__line {
    flex: 1 1 18rem;
    min-width: 0;
    color: var(--k-text-strong);
    font-size: var(--k-type-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Stops the button stretching to the row when the sentence wraps under it. */
  .cta__act {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
</style>
